* Unreleased

Hardening:
* Scalar multiplication no longer schedules a number of point operations
  that follows the multiplier: how many point additions and doublings
  signing, key generation, EdDSA signing and an ECDH exchange schedule
  is now derived from the public parameters of the curve alone -- from
  its order where the point carries one, and from its field where the
  point carries no order. This narrows the Minerva class timing
  side channel of CVE-2024-23342 (GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp,
  PYSEC-2026-1325), where the time a signature took followed the bit
  length of the per-signature nonce. A multiplier is normalised to the
  one odd representative of it that lies between that order and three
  times it, and recoded into a fixed length sequence of digits none of
  which is zero, so no digit is skipped and no iteration is spared. On
  NIST256p signing and key generation schedule 65 point additions and no
  doublings at all once the generator has built its precomputation
  table, whatever the nonce, where the older ladder ran over dozens of
  distinct counts; building that table, multiplying a point that carries
  no table as an ECDH exchange does, and each of the Edwards curves
  settle on fixed counts of their own in the same way.
* An ECDH exchange against a remote public point decoded from an
  encoding is covered as well, which is the key agreement case the
  advisory names. Such a point reports no order -- no encoding carries
  one and ``from_string()`` builds the point from coordinates alone -- so
  there is nothing to normalise the long term private key against, and
  earlier releases spent exactly the bit length of that key in doublings.
  The curve supplies the width instead: Hasse's theorem bounds the number
  of points of a curve over a field of p elements to within 2*sqrt(p) of
  p + 1, and the order of any point divides that number, so no order the
  curve can hold reaches 2**(bit_length(p) + 1). The multiplier is made
  odd by setting its lowest bit, recoded into the digit count that bound
  gives, and the point subtracted from the product of the odd form, both
  answers being derived for every multiplier and one picked out by index.
  On NIST256p such an exchange costs 73 point additions and 257 doublings
  whatever the private key is, one addition more than the 72 and 257 the
  same exchange costs against a public key this process derived from a
  ``SigningKey``, and a digit more on a curve whose group is narrower
  than its field -- 37 and 113 on SECP112r2.
* The nonce bit size hiding added in 0.14 was incomplete and this
  replaces it. The padding it applied when signing was taken off again
  by the reduction modulo twice the order of the curve that the
  multiplication performed, so it was without effect for a fraction of
  the nonces that depended on the curve: all but a vanishing fraction of
  them on SECP160r1, four in five on BRAINPOOLP384r1, one in two on
  BRAINPOOLP256r1. What the old ladder leaked besides was the Hamming
  weight of the NAF recoding of the multiplier, since it skipped an
  addition on every zero digit. The hiding now lives in the arithmetic
  alone, with nothing above it left to cancel it. EdDSA never went
  through that padding at all -- it was applied in
  ``ecdsa.ecdsa.Private_key.sign()`` and nowhere else -- and both
  Edwards curves are now covered by the same normalisation and recoding.
* The modular inversion of the nonce is performed on a blinded value and
  unblinded afterwards, so the iterations it takes no longer follow the
  nonce. The factor is drawn afresh for every signature, so what is
  inverted is unrelated to the nonce.
* Verification was never affected by this attack, its multipliers being
  public as soon as the signature is, and has deliberately been left
  alone: its combined multiplication is unchanged statement for
  statement, as are the addition and doubling formulas beneath it. It
  has always split into two separate multiplications where both of its
  points carry a precomputation table, so verification with a key
  ``precompute()`` was called on does now take the hardened ladder,
  reaching the same point from the same public multipliers; verification
  without one is untouched.
* Please note that this DOES NOT make the library secure against
  side-channel attacks (timing attacks); it is not a constant time
  implementation and is not claimed to be one. A residual timing signal
  remains, since Python integers are variable width and cost more to
  work with as they grow and the field arithmetic defers some reductions
  on purpose, so individual operations still differ in duration where
  their number no longer does, and nothing stronger than a measured
  reduction is attainable in pure Python. Power analysis,
  electromagnetic emanation and cache or other microarchitectural
  channels are not addressed at all.
* Three coverage limits are recorded rather than removed. At most two
  residues of any order end a ladder with its accumulator equal to the
  entry being added, which a twisted Edwards ladder pays for with one
  doubling beyond the schedule, so such a nonce costs one operation more
  than every other -- seven registered curves have two such residues,
  eighteen have one and SECP160r1 has none, a chance of about 2**-255 on
  a 256-bit curve. An order that is even, or below 17, cannot be recoded
  at all, and a point of one keeps the multiplication earlier releases
  performed; every registered curve declares an odd order of at least
  110 bits, so neither reaches one. That older ladder also answers a
  negative multiplier of a point with no order and one wider than every
  group of its curve, neither of which a private key or a nonce can be,
  both being drawn from [1, order), and a point with no order that is
  flagged as a curve generator, which no release since 0.14 will
  multiply at all.
* This is local hardening only and creates no upstream patched version
  marker: no release is recorded anywhere as fixing CVE-2024-23342, the
  advisory listing every version as affected and none as patched, so a
  vulnerability scanner will keep reporting it. ``SECURITY.md`` is the
  canonical statement of the countermeasure, its coverage, its
  compatibility notes and its residual risk.

Compatibility:
* Signatures are bit identical to the ones earlier releases produced,
  what the multiplication adds to a multiplier being a multiple of the
  order of the point, and deterministic RFC 6979 signatures stay
  reproducible byte for byte.
* Public call signatures, key encodings and signature encodings are
  unchanged, and no public return type changed.
* Three things did change that a caller can observe or depend on, and
  the three bullets below are the whole list.
* A multiplier is read for the integer it stands for before anything
  else a multiplication does with it, and one that stands for no integer
  is refused with a ``TypeError`` naming its type, ``multiplier must be
  an integer, not str`` and so on. The reading is ``operator.index()``
  first, which takes ``int``, ``bool`` and the ``mpz`` of both gmpy
  releases losslessly and reads only their type; then, for a value of
  another type, a conversion accepted only if the integer it yields
  still compares equal to it. A ``float``, ``Fraction`` or ``Decimal``
  with no fractional part therefore still answers with the same multiple
  earlier releases answered it with, and answers on the affine path as
  well, where reading the multiplier's bits directly used to raise for
  every float from two upwards. Whether anything is lost is asked of the
  value in its own type, so that refusing ``Decimal("1e1000000000")``
  costs no allocation of a billion digits; earlier releases refused it
  too, from the same ``decimal`` context but one digit of width later,
  which makes a ``Decimal`` of exactly that width the one value they
  answered correctly that is refused here. What those releases made
  something else of is what is refused: a fraction of one half was
  answered with the point and 2.5, 3.5 and 3.9 all with the triple, a
  ``complex`` of one with the point, and a ``None`` or an empty sequence
  was read as a zero and answered with the point at infinity, while a
  ``str`` or a ``list`` raised a message naming string formatting or the
  ``%`` operator rather than the multiplier, saying nothing of the
  multiplication. ``SECURITY.md`` gives that per path. Reading the
  multiplier first is also what keeps a float out of the arithmetic,
  where release 1.17 of the older gmpy binding answers one taken modulo
  a curve order by attempting an allocation of exabytes and aborting the
  interpreter. The refusal is deliberate: the shortcut that answers a
  multiplier of one had to move behind that reading step, or a nonce of
  one would never reach a ladder at all.
* A pickled point still writes the plain instance dictionary with the
  precomputation table in it, exactly what every release up to 0.19.1
  wrote, so the container did not move; its contents did. The table now
  holds, for each digit position, the odd multiples of the point a whole
  window can select, where earlier releases cached successive doublings,
  so compatibility runs one way only. A point unpickled with a table an
  earlier release wrote is accepted, that table being recognised as one
  of another layout, discarded and lazily rebuilt. The opposite
  direction is not guarded and cannot be: a state written here and read
  by a release older than the countermeasure hands it a table of the new
  layout to index as the successive doublings it expects, and it would
  answer with a wrong point rather than raise. That residual is accepted
  and recorded in ``SECURITY.md``.
* Signing draws one blinding factor for the modular inversion per
  signature and reads ``os.urandom()`` at least once to get it: the
  rejection sampling of ``util.randrange()`` reads it again for every
  draw it discards, and a factor that is not invertible modulo a
  composite generator order is drawn again as well. Earlier releases did
  not read it at all when the caller supplied the nonce or when the
  nonce was the deterministic one of RFC 6979. The draw is separate from
  the ``entropy=`` argument, which still feeds nonce generation only, the
  signature is unaffected, and a refusal of the source raises
  ``RuntimeError`` rather than dropping the blinding.

Performance:
* Signing got faster rather than slower, the fixed recoding costing
  fewer point additions on average than the ladder it replaces, and
  verification with a precomputed public key gains for the same reason,
  a combined multiplication delegating to that ladder when both of its
  points have a table. Verification without a precomputed key is the one
  multiplication path deliberately left alone and is unchanged.
* The deterministic part of that is the operation count, which
  ``test_side_channel.py`` asserts to exact numbers: a fixed 65 point
  additions on NIST256p, where the ladder it replaces took about twenty
  for a nonce a quarter of the full width and about ninety for a
  full-width one.
* Throughput itself is machine specific and no portable figure is offered
  here. For scale, from single ``speed.py`` runs on one machine under
  CPython 3.13 with the pure Python integer backend: roughly 2400
  NIST256p signatures per second against roughly 1900 for the code this
  replaces, and roughly 1380 precomputed verifications against roughly
  1020. Verification without precomputation came out near 500 either way.
  Those figures move by several percent between runs on one machine and
  more between machines, so measure your own rather than relying on them.
* One caution when benchmarking this: a comparison only means something
  over uniformly drawn multipliers. The old ladder skipped an addition
  for every zero digit, so a sparse multiplier such as a power of two, or
  a power of two plus one, took it only one or two additions and ran far
  faster than the fixed count now does. That spread, from a single
  addition to about a hundred on one curve, is the leak rather than a
  speed to measure against.

Maintenance:
* New ``src/ecdsa/test_side_channel.py`` holds the point operation
  counts above to exact numbers, so a regression there is a test
  failure. It runs as part of the normal test suite.
* New ``minerva_probe.py``, also reachable as ``tox -e leak``, measures
  the wall clock signal that remains, stratified by nonce bit length,
  with the statistical tests the external tlsfuzzer harness applies to
  the same question. It reports the earliest rejecting configured prefix
  of its prefix list, or that no rejection occurred at the tested
  prefixes through the largest of them; that is an observation about
  those prefixes and nothing more, from which no threshold is inferred.
  It is opt-in and deliberately not part of the default test run or of
  CI, a statistical timing measurement not being usable as a pass/fail
  gate. It exits 0 for a usable report, 1 if its own self-check failed,
  and 2 if it could not produce a report it is willing to stand behind.
  It certifies nothing.
* The test suite gained tests and lost none. No test that shipped in
  0.19.1 was removed, renamed, or had a line, a decorator, an example or
  an assertion of it changed.



* Release 0.19.1 (13 Mar 2025)

New API:
* ``der.remove_implitic`` and ``der.encode_implicit`` for decoding and
  encoding DER IMPLICIT values with custom tag values and arbitrary
  classes

Bug fixes:
* Minor fixes around arithmetic with curves that have non-prime order
  (useful for experimentation, not practical deployments)
* Fix arithmetic to work with curves that have (0, 0) on the curve
* Fix canonicalization of signatures when ``s`` is just slightly
  above half of curve order

Maintenance:
* Dropped official support for Python 3.5 (again, issues with CI, support
  for Python 2.6 and Python 2.7 is unchanged)
* Officialy support Python 3.12 and 3.13 (add them to CI)
* Removal of few more unnecessary `six.b` literals (Alexandre Detiste)
* Fix typos in warning messages


* Release 0.19.0 (08 Apr 2024)

New API:
* `to_ssh` in `VerifyingKey` and `SigningKey`, supports Ed25519 keys only
  (Pablo Mazzini)

New features:
* Support for twisted Brainpool curves

Doc fix:
* Fix curve equation in glossary
* Documentation for signature encoding and signature decoding functions

Maintenance:
* Dropped official support for 3.3 and 3.4 (because of problems running them
  in CI, not because it's actually incompatible; support for 2.6 and 2.7 is
  unaffected)
* Fixes aroung hypothesis parameters
* Officially support Python 3.11 and 3.12
* Small updates to test suite to make it work with 3.11 and 3.12 and new
  releases of test dependencies
* Dropped the internal `_rwlock` module as it's unused
* Added mutation testing to CI, lots of speed-ups to the test suite
  to make it happen
* Removal of unnecessary `six.b` literals (Alexandre Detiste)

Deprecations:
* `int_to_string`, `string_to_int`, and `digest_integer` from `ecdsa.ecdsa`
  module are now considered deprecated, they will be removed in a future
  release

